#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(5s + 4)x = 9x$\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n$ax = bx$\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx$. In this case,\n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 5 \\\\\ns &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110297 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12$\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r$?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x$, and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n$k + 4 = 6$.\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get\n$k|= 2$.\n- Equating the constants, we have\n$3r = 12$.\nSolving this equation for $r$, we get\n$r|= 4$.\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4$, we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n1. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n2. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n3. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$4x + 1|= 4x + 1; 4x + 1 - 4x|= 4x + 1 - 4x; 1|= 1$.\nThis equation is true for all values of $x$. Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$-3x|=-4x + 6; - 3x + 4x|=-4x + 6 + 4x; x|= 6$.\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6$.\n- For the third equation, we have\n$2x + 7|= 2x - 1; 2x + 7 - 2x|= 2x - 1 - 2x; 7|=-1$.\nThis equation is false for all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e44dcf-460e-54d3-a79a-545445d92869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108471} {:answer/id #uuid "9618c04e-6bf8-5f69-a644-698a27923a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108472} {:answer/id #uuid "1d24c4ff-5342-57d1-8ece-085b802a55e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108473} {:answer/id #uuid 